    authors: "Samirah Alkhudaydi, Abdullatif Alsulami, Stephanie McGrath and Julie A Moreno",
    body: "Alzheimer’s disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder characterized by the accumulation of misfolded proteins, including amyloid-β (Aβ₁₋₄₂), which disrupt neuronal function and lead to cognitive decline and reduced lifespan. This study investigated the therapeutic potential of Rapamycin (RAP), an FDA-approved pharmaceutical compound being repurposed for Alzheimer’s disease therapy due to its ability to attenuate proteotoxicity and oxidative stress in multiple model systems. Caenorhabditis elegans serves as a robust and genetically tractable model for studying Alzheimer’s disease mechanisms and therapeutic strategies, owing to its short lifespan, simple nervous system, and strong genetic conservation with humans. The mechanistic target of rapamycin (mTOR) pathway is a central regulator of aging, proteostasis, and neuronal survival. Its inhibition by rapamycin has demonstrated protective effects against neurodegeneration in several species. We hypothesized that rapamycin, through inhibition of mTOR signaling, would delay paralysis in the transgenic C. elegans strains, which expresses human Aβ₁₋₄₂ and models Alzheimer’s disease–like proteotoxic stress. Our results show that rapamycin treatment significantly delayed paralysis onset, suggesting a neuroprotective effect in the Alzheimer’s disease model. This study is among the first to systematically evaluate rapamycin across a range of doses in the C. elegans model, providing novel insight into its dose-dependent modulation of Aβ-induced toxicity. These findings underscore rapamycin’s potential as a therapeutic strategy for Alzheimer’s disease, supporting future exploration of combinational treatments and validation in higher model organisms.",
