    body: "Adrenocortical-associated endocrinopathy (AAE) is an incurable endocrine disorder affecting 70% of North American domestic ferrets (Mustela putorius furo). Clinical signs of AAE include alopecia, pruritus, vulvar swelling, and behavioral alterations. Most often associated with adrenal disease, if left untreated, AAE predisposes ferrets to potentially life-threatening complications such as estrogen-induced bone marrow suppression, anemia, and sepsis in females; and prostatic hyperplasia and urethral obstruction in males. The primary pathophysiological driver of adrenal disease is thought to be chronic overstimulation of luteinizing hormone (LH) from the pituitary gland. Historically, it was thought that early gonadectomy and the resulting loss of negative feedback on the hypothalamus led to LH overproduction, up-regulation of LH receptors in the adrenal glands, increased ectopic sex steroids, and adrenal dysregulation. However, the identification of adrenal disease in intact ferrets has led to other hypothesized etiologies, such as abnormal light cycles and genetics. Our aim is to utilize intact ferrets with clinical signs of adrenal disease and/or reproductive hormone profiles suggesting the presence of adrenal disease to advance our understanding of the physiology of AAE. Using luteinizing hormone releasing hormone, gonadectomy, ultrasound, hormone analysis, histopathology, and immunohistochemistry, we are investigating the sources of reproductive hormones and the role of LH and its receptors in development of the disease. By expanding our understanding of AAE etiology, this research could lead to further elucidation of the pathophysiology, and improve prevention and treatment of this important disease in domestic ferrets.",
