    title: "Effects of EIDD-2801 on clinical remission and calicivirus shedding on cats with chronic gingivostomatitis",
    authors: "JC Alvarado Colon, Christine Cordova, Brittany Lyle, Lupe Lopez, Jennifer Hawley, Petra Cerna, and Michael R. Lappin",
    body: "Feline Chronic Gingivostomatitis (FCGS) is a debilitating condition causing chronic oral inflammation in cats which leads to pain, anorexia, and decreased quality of life. Several suspected triggers with feline calicivirus (FCV), feline herpesvirus 1 (FHV-1), and feline immunodeficiency virus (FIV) being over-represented. Current treatment options, such as full-mouth dental extractions, are costly, invasive, and only moderately effective. There are multiple studies showing efficacy and safety of the RNA inhibitor EIDD-2801 for the treatment of feline infectious peritonitis virus infections.  FCV is also a RNA virus and so we hypothesized the EIDD-2801 would induce positive treatment responses in cats with FCGS.  Our first pilot study in 8 FCV-positive cats showed a variety of positive results.  That pilot data led to the project described here which was funded by the Morris Animal Foundation and approved by a Clinical Review Board. All cats in the current study were positive for FCV and were housed in the participating cat sanctuary in 2 colony rooms. A standardized stomatitis scoring rubric was applied to each cat by the sanctuary staff once weekly. Intra-oral photographs were made weekly to allow for inflammation score determination from afar by an observer masked to the treatment groups  Cats that randomized into the treatment group (n = 17) were administered EIDD-2801 at 13 - 15 mg/kg, PO, twice daily for 8 weeks and the 5 control cats were only administered supportive care.  A treatment outcome scale that ranged from 0 (no response) to 5 (complete resolution) was designed and then applied to the week 0 and week 8 stomatitis scoring data.  Overall, 10 of 17 (58.0%) of treated cats had moderate to complete resolution which was significantly different (P = 0.04) when compared to the control cats, none of which showed better than borderline improvement.  The study is ongoing.",
