    body: "Computer-assisted surgical navigation (CAS) has revolutionized human medicine but is only in its infancy in veterinary medicine. Understanding CAS in veterinary medicine may improve outcomes for patients, especially spinal procedures. This study aims to assess the feasibility and accuracy of the Synaptive Medical CAS system for equine cervical spine applications, specifically targeting procedures requiring precise localization.  Three (n = 3) equine cervical spine (C1–T1) cadavers were prepared, each with one fiducial array drilled into spinous process of C2 and fiducial markers distributed for landmarks registration. Computed tomography (CT) images enabled 3D reconstruction and intraoperative guidance. Pins were placed by four individuals with varying veterinary surgical experience at the intervertebral foramen at C2-C3, facet joint at C3-C4, and intervertebral disc at C3-C4. Procedure time was recorded for each participant.  Postoperative CT scans were obtained to calculate the Euclidean distance for accuracy assessment between the shortest distance of each pin and target. The overall average Euclidean distance was 11.33 ± 11.02 mm.  The average Euclidean distance was 10.26 ± 14.53 for the intervertebral foramen, 12.48 mm ± 8.12 for the facet joint, and 11.16 mm ± 10.72 for the intervertebral disc.  Statistical significance was observed in duration between participants but not in accuracy. Despite limitations, this study provides evidence supporting the efficacy of surgical navigation using SMS in non-cranial areas. Findings also suggest that SMS may serve as an accessible tool with potential usability independent of operator experience. Overall, CAS may be a feasible and minimally invasive approach for equine cervical spinal procedures.",
