    title: "NBS1 Deficiency Enhances Sensitivity to ATR Inhibition by Impairing the DNA Damage Response",
    authors: "Gamze Badakul, Junko Maeda, and Takamitsu A Kato",
    body: "Nijmegen Breakage Syndrome (NBS) is a rare autosomal recessive disorder caused by mutations in the NBN gene, which encodes nibrin (NBS1), a key component of the MRN complex (MRE11–RAD50–NBS1). Loss of NBS1 disrupts MRN complex stability, leading to chromosomal instability, radiosensitivity, immunodeficiency, and cancer predisposition. The MRN complex is essential for sensing DNA double-strand breaks (DSBs), activating ATM kinase, and mediating DNA repair through non-homologous end joining (NHEJ) or homologous recombination (HR). Beyond its ATM-dependent roles, NBS1 contributes to ATR signaling, G2/M checkpoint regulation, and genome stability, thereby influencing cancer susceptibility. This study aimed to investigate the non-classical functions of NBS1 in ATR signaling and cell cycle regulation, DNA damage response defects, and therapeutic vulnerabilities using CRISPR/Cas9-mediated NBN knockout Chinese hamster (V79) cell lines. The NBS1-deficient V79 cells exhibited slower proliferation, increased genomic instability, and greater sensitivity to γ-irradiation compared with wild-type cells, though no difference in plating efficiency or mutation frequency was observed. Cytogenetic analyses, including premature chromosome condensation (PCC), chromosome aberrations, and mitotic index evaluation, confirmed impaired DNA repair and checkpoint defects. NBS1-deficient cells displayed heightened sensitivity to DNA-damaging agents such as camptothecin, cisplatin, etoposide, mitomycin C, and methyl methanesulfonate, as well as inhibitors targeting ATM, ATR, DNA-PK, and PARP. However, these cells were not sensitive to bleomycin, hydroxyurea, or UV-C, differing from previous reports. Combination treatments with the ATR inhibitor VE-821 and genotoxic agents (camptothecin, etoposide, or γ-rays) produced synergistic cytotoxicity in NBS1-deficient cells. These findings underscore the compensatory role of ATR signaling in maintaining genome integrity in the absence of NBS1 and suggest that co-targeting ATR with DNA-damaging therapies may offer a promising strategy for NBS-related malignancies.",
